Bagels told the audient void (and rec.games.roguelike.nethack):
>  A question to everyone here: why do you play NetHack?

I started playing 'cos it came with Debian, could be played in a
text terminal, could be dived into without reading the fine manual
(despite recommendations), and promised much timewasting and silliness.

> Particularly, why 
>  do you play it over servers (like nethack.alt.org)?

Partly because the lag makes me play a little more slowly and think more
carefully about what I'm doing.  Partly for the ability to loot bones
piles for stuff that I'd never encounter in my own style of play.
Partly to show off.

>  In addition, I'm particularly curious about is this: how have other 
>  players helped you (or hindered you) in your games?

The bones piles can be a god-send when I need one particular item,
though they can be annoying if encountered before the first
identification spree.

There are also tricks I've learned from watching other players, usually
involving object interactions that I must have been aware of but never
thought of a sensible use for (such as using suspicious Sokoban boulders
to identify certain rings).

The one time someone sent me a message while playing, it was to remind
me that there was a throne under that chest (I was leaving the Knight
Quest), which wasn't especially useful for a genoless wishless
character.

